sting lock was treated as the caller's own:\n\n- **`src/commands/includes/removeLock.lua`**: released the lock whenever one existed. This helper is shared by complete/fail, immediate retry, delayed retry, move-back-to-wait and move-to-waiting-children, so all of them let a stale worker through.\n- **`src/commands/extendLock-2.lua`**: only checked that the lock existed, then overwrote it with the caller's token.\n- **`src/commands/extendLocks-1.lua`**: same problem in the batch renewal path.\n\n## Fix\nOne line in each file, restoring the owner comparison. No TypeScript, test, package or config changes.\n\n- **Stale operations**: rejected with the existing `Lock mismatch for job …` error (code -6) before anything is written, so state, attempt counters, result, owner lock and events are untouched.\n- **Stale renewals**: single-job renewal returns `0` and batch renewal reports the job id as failed, as the existing API does.\n- **Missing locks**: still return the `Missing lock` error (code -2).\n- **Administrative calls**: the token `\"0\"` path that skips the lock check is unchanged.\n\n## Verification\nI added `tests/stale_worker_ownership.redis.test.ts` (8 tests). It makes a worker lose its lock, recovers the job through the real stalled-job check, has a second worker claim it, and then tries each stale operation.\n\n- Each rejection test compares job state, the job hash, the lock holder, event stream length and queue counts before and after.\n- Without the fix, 7 of the 8 new tests fail; the one that passes either way is the missing-lock check.\n- The tests also confirm the current owner can still renew and finish the job, and that retry limits and fixed backoff still apply for the owner.\n\nThe PostgreSQL backend was not run; I only read its batch-renewal query, which already compares the token. Nothing is committed.","ttft_ms":2783,"type":"result","duration_ms":236881,"uuid":"e86a27ca-cfdd-40a5-9ac7-a8872a5684ac","ttft_stream_ms":1437,"time_to_request_ms":24,"firs